===Democratic-held seats===
On March 17, 2025, the [[National Republican Congressional Committee]] released their target seat list which included Democratic-held seats and open seats.<ref>{{cite web |last=Marinella |first=Mike |date=2025-03-17 |title=NRCC Targets 26 Offensive Seats to Expand House Majority |url=https://www.nrcc.org/2025/03/17/nrcc-targets-26-offensive-seats-to-expand-house-majority/ |access-date=2025-08-25 |website=NRCC |language=en-US}}</ref> On September 18, 2025, this list was revised adding three districts (TX-9, TX-32, and TX-35).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Marinella |first=Mike |date=2025-09-18 |title=NRCC Expands Target List with Three Texas Opportunities to Grow GOP Majority |url=https://www.nrcc.org/2025/09/18/nrcc-expands-target-list-with-three-texas-opportunities-to-grow-gop-majority/ |access-date=2026-05-31 |website=NRCC |language=en-US}}</ref>  It was revised again on July 20, 2026, where eight more districts (AL-2, FL-14, FL-22, FL-25, LA-6, MO-5, OH-1, and TN-9) were added and one district (FL-23) was removed.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Marinella |first=Mike |date=2026-07-20 |title=NRCC Expands Offensive Target List to Grow GOP Majority |url=https://www.nrcc.org/2026/07/20/nrcc-expands-offensive-target-list-to-grow-gop-majority/ |access-date=2026-07-22 |website=NRCC |language=en-US}}</ref> The [[Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee]] also released its frontline members, which are bolded below, and the ones not bolded are seats targeted by the Republican committee but not in the Democratic committee frontline program:

